@FacetPathKind Type InfallibleStructural
@FacetPathKind Type FallibleStructural
@FacetPathKind Type VariantPath

@FacetPathKind Type ReadablePath =
  InfallibleStructural | FallibleStructural | VariantPath
@FacetPathKind Type WritablePath =
  InfallibleStructural | FallibleStructural | VariantPath
@FacetPathKind Type PutPath = InfallibleStructural
@FacetPathKind Type PreviewPath = VariantPath
@FacetPathKind Type CasePath = VariantPath

@doc """
Standard `Facet` type declaration.
`Facet<K, S, A, T, B>` is a compiler-managed path capability from source `S`
to focus `A`; `K` is its compiler-derived path kind and `T`/`B` are the
updated source/focus types. Use `_, _` for deferred update slots.
Facet paths are compile-time capabilities: bind them locally when useful, then
consume them in that lexical scope or an inner closure via
`Facet::view/preview/put/set/over/over_result/case_set/case_over`.

Use `Facet` when you want to describe "where" inside a value the compiler should
look or update.
`Option<T>` fields work naturally with enum-case Facet paths such as
`Option.Some`, `case_set`, and `case_over`.
When a field should participate in Result-style update flows, use
`Result<T>` explicitly. Represent a missing value with `Err(NoneError)`.

- `Type.segment` builds a path itself
- `value.segment` is sugar for applying the corresponding path to `value`

Common path forms:

- struct / record field: `User.name`
- inferred unary capture from context: `_.name`
- explicit type-root capture in unary function context: `&User.name`
- tuple root path: `Tuple._0`, `Tuple._1`, ...
- list root path: `List.[1]`
- negative list root path: `List.[-1]`
- list range root path: `List.[1..-1]`
- dynamic list root path: `List.[index + 1]`
- map root path: `HashMap.["talk"]`
- dynamic map root path: `HashMap.[String::trim(name)]`
- value-side list/map sugar: `values.[1]`, `map.["talk"]`
- dynamic value-side list/map sugar: `values.[index]`, `map.[name]`
- property list segment: `User.scores.[1]`
- property map segment: `User.score.["talk"]`
- dynamic property segment: `User.scores.[index]`, `User.score.[name]`
- range property segment: `User.scores.[1..-1]`
- nested path: `User.profile -> Profile.name`, `User.profile.name`
- function-form nested path: `Facet::compose(User.profile, Profile.name)`
- enum variant selector: `Expr.Add`
- value sugar: `value.segment`
- shorthand source/path capture: `~value.segment`

`User.name` is the Facet path itself.
`_.name` infers the source type from unary function context and uses the same
field / Facet traversal rules.
`&User.name` captures that type-root path as a unary function `User -> String`.
Roots that do not determine the full receiver type require callable context.
For example:

`users |*> _.name`
`users |*> &User.name`

Use `_.path` when the surrounding context already determines the source type.
Use `&Type.path` when you want to make that source type explicit in the code.

`~value.segment` is compiler-managed shorthand that expands to a source value and
structural path when consumed by
`Facet::view/preview/put/set/over/over_result/case_set/case_over`.
The source expression is evaluated exactly once.
It is never a first-class `Facet` value, and API-specific validity is still
decided by the consuming `Facet` operation.

`Facet` can be bound locally and consumed through lexical references:

`facet = User.name`
`tuple_facet = Tuple._1`

An inner closure may use an outer path binding in a Facet API. The path itself
is injected at compile time, not stored as a runtime capture. Ordinary index/key
data may be captured lexically. A Facet cannot be passed to a non-Facet function,
returned from a function or closure, used as a closure parameter, or stored inside
containers such as `List`, tuples, `Ok(...)`, or `Err(...)`.

## REPL inspection

`:doc Ty` shows the declaration documentation for a type. Use `:doc Facet.Ty`
for the generic Facet-root explanation, `:info Ty` for its field policies, and
`:facet Ty.path` for one path's structural details.

`:info Ty` is definition inspection and includes private fields. `:facet` can
inspect a private path such as `User.password` and reports that it is unavailable
to the current REPL scope; this does not make the path usable in ordinary source.
Fields are not standalone documentation or signature targets, so `:doc Ty.field`
and `:sig Ty.field` guide to `:info Ty` or `:facet Ty.field`.

Private field paths are validated when a facet path segment is formed.
Outside the owning `impl Type` / `impl Trait for Type`, both `User.secret` and
`value.secret` are compile errors.

Readonly follows a different rule boundary from private visibility.

- `readonly field` path segments may still be formed and viewed
- readonly is checked when mutating Facet operations are applied
- owner code may replace a readonly field itself
- writable traversal through a readonly field is rejected
- `@readonly defstruct` marks every field readonly; owner replacement follows the same rules
- explicit public fields in an `@readonly defstruct` declaration are rejected

Container bracket expressions follow these additional rules:

- `List.[expr]` requires plain `Int`
- `List.[start..end]` requires plain `Int` endpoints and focuses `List<T>`
- negative list indexes and range endpoints are resolved from the tail: `-1`
  means the last element
- `HashMap.[expr]` requires plain `String`
- `Result<Int>` and `Result<String>` are rejected; unwrap before building the path
- `put` still accepts only infallible structural paths, so list/map bracket
  segments are excluded even when `expr` itself is pure
- `const Facet<...>` accepts only literal bracket segments such as `List.[0]`,
  `List.[-1]`, `List.[1..3]`, and `HashMap.["talk"]`
"""

@builtin type Facet<$K, $S, $A, $T, $B>

@autoimport
impl Facet {
  @doc """
  View the focus at `facet` from `source`.
  The declared contract is `Result`-shaped; the compiler refines this based on
  path context.

  How to read the return type:

  - plain field / tuple paths on plain values return plain `A`
  - if `source` is already `Result<S>`, the result stays `Result<A>`
  - if the path contains an enum variant selector such as `Expr.Add`, list
    index, or HashMap key, the result is promoted to `Result<A>`

  `value.segment` is shorthand for the same read:
  `cfg.entrypoint`
  `pair._0`

  Variant paths are fallible because the runtime value may be a different
  variant:

  When the selector does not match, the result is `Err(FacetReadVariantMismatch(...))`.
  List and HashMap path segments are also fallible: they return
  `FacetListIndexOutOfBounds` or `FacetKeyNotFound` through `Result`.
  Their bracket expression may be any ordinary runtime expression as long as it
  resolves to plain `Int` or `String`. List ranges use inclusive endpoints and
  return a `List<T>` focus.
  The bracket expression is evaluated exactly once per Facet operation.

  `Facet::view` may cross readonly fields and readonly struct boundaries because
  readonly is enforced only for mutating Facet operations.

  Capturing `view` infers the source from a concrete path before checking its
  root and segments. `&Facet::view(Duration.millis, &1)`, `&Duration.millis`,
  and `&p` for `p = Duration.millis` all have type `Duration -> Int`.
  Autoimported `view` uses the same resolved API. Conflicting annotations are
  rejected. Tuple/List/HashMap roots still require sufficient type context.
  Other Facet APIs use ordinary capture inference. A placeholder cannot
  receive a FacetPath itself, including a path operand in composition.
  Placeholders inside `[expr]` are ordinary index/key/range endpoint data
  and are allowed under the usual bracket type rules.
  Bracket expressions in a read capture are evaluated once per call.
  Inspect shows `FnCapture(module: Facet, name: view, sig: (Duration -> Int))`.

  ## REPL
  xldr(1)> read = &Facet::view(Duration.millis, &1)
  > read: (Duration -> Int) = FnCapture(module: Facet, name: view, sig: (Duration -> Int))
  xldr(2)> read(10ms)
  10

  xldr(1)> cfg = Config::new("demo")
  > cfg: Config = Config { name: "demo", entrypoint: "Main::main", paths: [] }
  xldr(2)> cfg.entrypoint
  "Main::main"

  xldr(1)> pair = ("alice", 42)
  > pair: (String, Int) = ("alice", 42)
  xldr(2)> pair._0
  "alice"

  xldr(1)> pair = ("alice", 42)
  > pair: (String, Int) = ("alice", 42)
  xldr(2)> Facet::view(~pair._0)
  "alice"

  xldr(1)> Facet::view(BitWidth.Any, BitWidth::Any(5))
  Ok(5)
  """
  @builtin def view(facet: Facet<ReadablePath, $S, $A, _, _>, source: $S) -> Result<$A>

  @doc """
  Preview the focus at a variant Facet from `source`.
  `preview` is the explicit API for enum variant paths such as `Shape.Point`
  or `Option.Some`.

  Unlike `view`, `preview` rejects structural-only paths at compile time.
  Use `Facet::view` for field and tuple paths that do not pass through an enum
  variant.

  `Result` is not treated as an enum variant Facet here. A `Result<$S>` source
  remains the Facet execution context: `Ok(value)` continues through the path,
  and `Err(error)` propagates unchanged.
  """
  @builtin def preview(facet: Facet<PreviewPath, $S, $A, _, _>, source: $S) -> Result<$A>

  @doc """
  Put `value` at `facet` in plain `source`.
  Returns the updated source directly, without wrapping it in `Result`.

  `put` is the non-fallible companion to `set`.
  It is available only when both of these hold:

  - `source` is a plain value, not `Result<$S>`
  - `facet` is infallible structural-only (field / tuple path, no variant
    selector, list index, or map key)

  The same readonly restrictions as `set` apply here.

  Use `set` instead when the update should compose through `Result` context or
  variant matching.
  List and HashMap path segments return `FacetListIndexOutOfBounds` or `FacetKeyNotFound`
  through `Result`, and are therefore not accepted by `put`.

  ## REPL
  xldr(1)> pair = ("alice", 42)
  > pair: (String, Int) = ("alice", 42)
  xldr(2)> Facet::put(Tuple._1, pair, 99)
  ("alice", 99)
  xldr(3)> Facet::put(~pair._1, 99)
  ("alice", 99)
  """
  @builtin def put(facet: Facet<PutPath, $S, $A, $T, $B>, source: $S, value: $B) -> $T

  @doc """
  Replace the focus at `facet` in `source` with `value`.
  Returns the updated source wrapped in `Result`.

  Use `set` when you already have the replacement value.
  `set` always receives the replacement focus type exactly. It does not wrap a
  plain payload in `Ok(...)`; use `over` to update a Result payload.

  Readonly rules:

  - readonly field traversal is rejected for mutation
  - owner code may replace the readonly field itself when the path ends there
  - `@readonly defstruct` applies these same rules to every field

  For nested updates, compose the path first:

  If the path contains a variant selector, `set` fails with
  `Err(FacetReadVariantMismatch(...))` when the current value is not on that variant.
  List and HashMap bracket segments reuse the same evaluated index/key for both
  the read and rebuild phases of one update.

  ## REPL
  xldr(1)> cfg = Config::new("demo")
  > cfg: Config = Config { name: "demo", entrypoint: "Main::main", paths: [] }
  xldr(2)> cfg2 =? Facet::set(Config.entrypoint, cfg, "App::main")
  > cfg2: Config = Config { name: "demo", entrypoint: "App::main", paths: [] }

  xldr(1)> pair = ("alice", 42)
  > pair: (String, Int) = ("alice", 42)
  xldr(2)> pair2 =? Facet::set(Tuple._1, pair, 99)
  > pair2: (String, Int) = ("alice", 99)
  xldr(3)> pair3 =? Facet::set(~pair._1, 99)
  > pair3: (String, Int) = ("alice", 99)

  xldr(1)> bw2 =? Facet::set(BitWidth.Any, BitWidth::Any(5), 7)
  > bw2: BitWidth = BitWidth::Any(7)
  """
  @builtin def set(facet: Facet<WritablePath, $S, $A, $T, $B>, source: $S, value: $B) -> Result<$T>

  @doc """
  Replace the payload of the final enum case at `facet`.
  `case_set` is valid only when the Facet path contains an enum selector and
  the final segment is that enum case.

  Enum segments are required and return `Err(FacetReadVariantMismatch(...))` when the
  source is on another variant.
  """
  @builtin def case_set(facet: Facet<CasePath, $S, $A, $T, $B>, source: $S, value: $B) -> Result<$T>

  @doc """
  Update the focus at `facet` in `source` with `update_fun`.
  `update_fun` receives the current focus and returns `Result<$A>`.

  Use `over` when the new value depends on the current focused value.
  When the focus is `Result<$A>`, `over` updates only the successful payload:
  `Ok(value)` calls the updater with `value`, while `Err(err)` skips the updater
  and leaves the field unchanged.
  This makes `Result`-backed fields compose directly with ordinary
  `A -> Result<A>` update helpers.

  The same readonly restrictions as `set` apply here.

  `update_fun` must return `Result`.
  If `update_fun` returns `Err(...)`, that error is propagated as-is.
  Dynamic list/map bracket expressions are evaluated once before the update and
  then reused throughout that operation.

  ## REPL
  xldr(1)> cfg = Config::new("demo")
  > cfg: Config = Config { name: "demo", entrypoint: "Main::main", paths: [] }
  xldr(2)> cfg2 =? Facet::over(Config.entrypoint, cfg, {|entry| Ok(entry ++ "!")})
  > cfg2: Config = Config { name: "demo", entrypoint: "Main::main!", paths: [] }

  xldr(1)> pair = ("alice", 42)
  > pair: (String, Int) = ("alice", 42)
  xldr(2)> pair2 =? Facet::over(Tuple._1, pair, {|n| Ok(n + 1)})
  > pair2: (String, Int) = ("alice", 43)
  xldr(3)> pair3 =? Facet::over(~pair._1, {|n| Ok(n + 1)})
  > pair3: (String, Int) = ("alice", 43)

  xldr(1)> bw2 =? Facet::over(BitWidth.Any, BitWidth::Any(7), {|n| Ok(n + 1)})
  > bw2: BitWidth = BitWidth::Any(8)
  """
  @builtin def over(facet: Facet<WritablePath, $S, $A, $T, $B>, source: $S, update_fun: ($A -> Result<$B>)) -> Result<$T>

  @doc """
  Update a `Result<$A>` focus as a whole.
  `update_fun` receives the entire current `Result<$A>` and returns
  `Result<Result<$A>>`.

  Use `over_result` when you want to rewrite `Ok(...)` and `Err(...)` directly.
  Use plain `over` when you only want to transform the successful payload.

  `update_fun` must return `Result`.
  If `update_fun` returns `Err(...)`, that error is propagated as-is.

  The same readonly restrictions as `set` apply here.

  `~value.segment` shorthand may also be used here for structural value paths.
  Dynamic list/map bracket expressions follow the same type rules as `view` and
  `over`.
  """
  @builtin def over_result(facet: Facet<WritablePath, $S, Result<$A>, $T, Result<$B>>, source: $S, update_fun: (Result<$A> -> Result<Result<$B>>)) -> Result<$T>

  @doc """
  Update a focus reached through an enum case path.
  `case_over` requires at least one required enum selector in the Facet path.

  `case_over` accepts both plain and `Result` focus.
  When the focus is plain `$A`, `update_fun` receives `$A` and returns
  `Result<$A>`.
  When the focus is `Result<$A>`, `update_fun` may either receive the `Ok`
  payload as `$A` or the whole `Result<$A>`.
  Payload-style updaters skip `Err(err)` and leave that branch unchanged.
  """
  @builtin def case_over(facet: Facet<CasePath, $S, $A, $T, $B>, source: $S, update_fun: ($A -> Result<$B>)) -> Result<$T>

  @doc """
  Bulk update special form.

  `Facet::bulk_update(source) { ... }` is a language-provided update form for
  applying multiple Facet-based updates to one state value in source order.

  The keyword forms `bulk_update(source) { ... }` and
  `bulk_update(source, { ... })` have the same meaning. The existing qualified
  form `Facet::bulk_update(source) { ... }` remains available; its block can
  also be placed inside the parentheses as `Facet::bulk_update(source, { ... })`.
  Parentheses around the source argument are required; `bulk_update source
  { ... }` is invalid. `bulk_update` is a keyword and cannot be shadowed by a
  declaration or binding. The source is evaluated once in every form.

  Each entry targets a relative path from `source` and must use one of these
  update shapes:

  - `path <- set(value)`
  - `path <- over(update_fun)`
  - `path <- over_result(update_fun)`
  - `path <- case_set(payload)`
  - `path <- case_over(update_fun)`
  - `path { nested_entries... }`

  Nested blocks extend the current path prefix. This makes the following forms
  equivalent:

  `address.country <- set("Tokyo")`
  `address { country <- set("Tokyo") }`

  `bulk_update` is not an ordinary callable block argument. The update block is
  a dedicated special-form surface, similar to `match`.
  Entries are separated by newlines, not commas.
  This is intentional for the qualified form as well: `Facet::bulk_update`
  selects the syntax and is not resolved as an ordinary outer function value.
  Moving the update block inside parentheses does not make it a block value
  or make `bulk_update` available as a first-class function.

  `bulk_update` lowers to ordinary `Facet::set` / `Facet::over` /
  `Facet::over_result` / `Facet::case_set` / `Facet::case_over` calls and
  returns `Result<$S>`.
  Each later entry sees the latest updated state produced so far.
  Update failures propagate as `Err`; they are not replaced with success
  values. Resolution or type errors in the generated Facet calls remain
  compile errors.
  List / HashMap bracket segments use the same dynamic-expression rules as
  ordinary Facet APIs, including plain `Int` / `String` requirements.

  Use ordinary functions and `|>=` outside `bulk_update` when you need whole
  state updaters of shape `$S -> Result<$S>`.

  ## Examples
  renamed =? Facet::bulk_update(user) {
    name <- set("taro")
    age <- over({|age| Ok(age + 1)})
  }

  rewritten =? Facet::bulk_update(account) {
    score <- over_result({|score: Result<Int>| Ok(Ok(9))})
  }

  moved =? Facet::bulk_update(user) {
    address {
      country <- set("Tokyo")
    }
  }

  scores2 =? Facet::bulk_update(user) {
    scores.[index + 1] <- over({|score| Ok(score + 10)})
    score_by_kind.[kind] <- set(9)
  }
  """
  @intrinsic def bulk_update(source: $S, updates: BulkUpdateEntries<$S>) -> Result<$S>

  @doc """
  Compose two facet paths in sequence.
  `compose(outer, inner)` focuses from `S` to `B` via `A`.
  `outer -> inner` is equivalent fixed syntax, also within `bulk_update`.
  Both operands must be independent Facet paths. This syntax is not extensible
  through user trait implementations.
  Dot access and calls complete each operand first; `->` associates to the left
  and binds more strongly than ordinary expression operators.
  The arrow in function and return types keeps its existing meaning.
  Capture boundaries are unchanged: use `&User.profile.name` to capture the
  entire nested path, not `&User.profile -> Profile.name`.

  Use this for nested access and updates.

  The types must line up:

  - `outer: Facet<K, S, A, _, _>`
  - `inner: Facet<L, A, B, _, _>`
  - result: `Facet<K, S, B, _, _>`

  Both arguments are FacetPaths and cannot be replaced by capture placeholders.
  Their bracket expressions may contain ordinary data placeholders.
  `compose` cannot be captured as a callable because it returns a FacetPath.
  A closure may compose lexical paths
  and consume the result in a Facet API, but cannot return the composed path.

  This is the canonical path-composition API.

  Private field segments keep their visibility rules after composition.
  If one segment is private and the current scope cannot see it, the composed
  path is rejected at compile time.

  ## REPL
  For a user-defined nested type, use the same shape as:
  outer = User.profile
  inner = Profile.name
  path2 = outer -> inner
  path = Facet::compose(outer, inner)
  """
  @builtin def compose(outer: Facet<$K, $S, $A, _, _>, inner: Facet<$L, $A, $B, _, _>) -> Facet<$K, $S, $B, _, _>
}
